

                
                    Best of GEnie..... September 1989
          News from the GEnie Forth RoundTable by Gary Smith

   As promised at the close of last issue's 'Best of GEnie' this time we
will re-examine ForthNet. ForthNet is a virtual Forth network that links
designated message bases of several Bulletin Boards and Informattion Services
in an attempt to provide greater distribution of Forth related information.
It is provided courtesy of the SysOps of its various links. Readers of this 
column may recall ForthNet was talked of as a dream yet unfulfilled in 'Forth
Dimensions' Volume X, Number 4. At that time, there was cause to wonder if it 
would even survive. Some questions still remain, serious questions, but as the 
evolution continues it is increasingly eveident a purpose is being served; and 
where there is purpose there is often will to succeed.
   
   For historical perspective ForthNet was a term coined in early 1986 when
the Forth Interest Group was still searching for a home for its electronic
message base that would provide easier and greater access than the FIGTree
BBS. Discussions at that time were being conducted on Delphi (tm), and 
consisted primarily of establishing guidelines and expectations. After the
GEnie(tm) Forth RoundTable was established and I was included as one of the
co-SysOps I once again began to explore the possibility of linking with other
Forth message bases. With the encouragment of GEnie lead SysOp Dennis Ruffer
and considerable help of Jack Woehr (jax) we established the first link with
the GEnie Forth RoundTable and one topic of the Well forth.conf which Jack is
fairwitness of. Later, I brought forth.conf on Wetware Unix BBS into this
link.

   Concurrently Jerry Shifrin had established the East Coast Forth Board as
a premier source of Forth information exchange, and had linked it via PCBoard
to West Coast Forth Board and later North Coast Forth Board. West Coast and
North Coast have both since been dissolved by their operators, but in their
places have risen British Columbia Forth Board and Real-Time Control Forth
Board, both linked to Jerry's East Coast Forth Board. Also linked to this
group is Metrolink Bulletin Boards which themselves maintain a loose network
similar to Opus and Fido. 

   It was the joining  of these two virtual nets into one ForthNet that has
resulted in a great single message base for all Forth users. For this much
credit must go to Jerry Shifrin. I was initially porting messages of
interest from Don Madsen's North Coast Forth Board to GEnie. This helped
extend the information input, but the circle was only half complete until
Jerry developed methods to make the message bases flow to the East Coast
Forth Board (and the other xCFB nodes) from GEnie and thereby finish the 
circuit that everything became a homogeneous message base whereby all users
of all services are literally able to communicate with one another. This has
been an exciting development. Now Forth programmers are able to tap a very
large pool of talent to help resolve problems. A new user of Forth encountering
an editor problem received seven responses from four sources in two days. That
is networking at its best. As a GEnie Forth RoundTable SysOp I will remind you
all of this is available to you simply by logging onto the GEnie Forth
RoundTable. You need not worry about the mechanics of ForthNet. All that is
asked in return is your participation. Nothing could be easier.

   All is not sunshine and roses, however. Much of the porting is still done
by one or two individuals. The absence of either shows immediately. This is
the bane of most virtual networks, though. Usenet functions only by the
grace of the volunteer postmasters at its various Unix sites, which is part
of the reason links in that system come and go. We hope to emulate their
success and have more links come into ForthNet than go.

   Exciting trends and possibilities are afoot. We are  currently establishing
a link in Australia on Lance Collins BBS for the PCBoards. We are also trying
to establish a Usenet link with other Forth sources with that common link. We
are porting messages into GEnie for redistribution from Usenet comp.lang.forth
and creating an edited ForthNet packet for distribution on Usenet. We have
been in touch with several Forth users we previously would have had no real
hope of establishing contact with. We want to encourage all who have
contacted us to continue to do so, and those who have not to please do so.
Jack Woehr (jax) jax@well.UUCP, jax@chariot.UUCP or myself (gars) at 
well!gars@lll-winken.arpa (this also works for jax), gars@wet.UUCP or 
gars@chinet.UUCP. Each new contact is like a new discovery.

